+# Decision: Cache Replacement Policy for the Preview Worker's Cache
+
+## Context
+
+The preview worker keeps a private cache of rendered contact sheets, bounded at 12 GiB of JPEG data. The workload is not uniform: editors alternate long sequential screenings, which sweep through thousands of one-use sheets, with comparison sessions that revisit a small set of clips over and over. A replacement policy therefore has to hold two things in tension at once — keep the sheets editors keep coming back to warm, and keep shedding the sheets that a scan has already moved past.
+
+The policy is invisible outside the worker. The review API forwards preview requests and preserves whatever outcome the worker returns (200 with JPEG bytes, 202 with a job ticket, 404 for an unknown revision); it never inspects cache state, and no editor-visible signal of priority is available to it. The decision is therefore wholly contained in the preview worker, and it does not alter the request contract.
+
+What is at stake: if the policy protects the wrong thing, editors lose the ability to compare clips they are actively comparing (a re-render costs about 450 ms and a second round trip); if it costs too much per request, the worker's own latency budget under 24 concurrent editors erodes; and if it cannot be explained to an operator reading the worker's metrics, it will be misdiagnosed when it misbehaves.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+### Gates
+
+- **Replacement bookkeeping stays private to the preview worker.** No other node may pin entries, signal priority, or depend on an entry remaining warm. *(inherited — the worker's boundary and the project's private-cache constraint.)*
+- **The policy keeps cached JPEG data within 12 GiB on a single worker.** No eviction work is delegated to shared or distributed coordination. *(inherited — the cache size bound and the single-worker scope of the pilot.)*
+
+### Weighted criteria
+
+- **Protect revisited sheets during a long screening** *(major)* — The primary purpose of the policy. A sequential screening visits sheets in order; sheets that editors come back to must survive it.
+- **Bookkeeping cost per request** *(moderate)* — Memory, CPU, and latency added to each cache hit by maintaining the policy's state.
+- **Follow a shift in editors' attention** *(moderate)* — How quickly the warm set moves when the editors move from one production to another.
+- **Robustness to a badly chosen parameter** *(moderate)* — Whether a mis-set policy degrades visibly or silently.
+- **Explicitness of the warm set** *(minor)* — How readily an operator can see what is cached and why.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Single-queue least-recently-used
+
+**Description.** Every cached sheet sits in one queue ordered by when it was last served. When the cache must make room, the sheet that has gone longest without being served is dropped. Serving a sheet moves it to the front of that order. This is the default replacement behaviour of a page cache and needs one piece of state per entry: when it was last used.
+
+**Demonstration.** An editor screens 4,000 clips in order, then returns to a comparison set of six clips opened an hour earlier. Each screened clip was served more recently than any comparison clip, so the six comparison sheets are the least recently used and are the first to be dropped.
+
+**Analysis.**
+- *Private bookkeeping* — passes; the queue is entirely inside the worker.
+- *12 GiB bound on one worker* — passes.
+- *Protect revisited sheets during a long screening* — **weak**. A scan makes every sheet it touches the most recently used one, so the sheets an editor will return to are exactly the ones a scan pushes to the back of the order.
+- *Bookkeeping cost per request* — **strong**. One recency update per hit and one comparison per eviction.
+- *Follow a shift in editors' attention* — **strong**. Recency follows current work immediately, with nothing to decay.
+- *Robustness to a badly chosen parameter* — **strong**. The only parameter is the cache size itself, and an incorrect value degrades in an obvious way (a cache that is too small).
+- *Explicitness of the warm set* — **strong**. One order explains every eviction.
+
+**Summary.** The cheapest policy to run and the easiest to explain, and the one that gives up the protection editors asked for.
+
+### Frequency counts with aging
+
+**Description.** Every cached sheet carries a reference count. When the cache must make room, the sheets with the lowest counts are dropped. Counts are periodically aged — halved or reset on a schedule — so that a sheet's popularity reflects recent activity rather than all history. This is the shape of a small-LFU cache with an aging window.
+
+**Demonstration.** A comparison set of six clips, each viewed a dozen times during a session, outranks the 4,000 clips the scan touched once. When the editors move to a different production, the old session's counts persist until they age out, so the old favourites continue to displace new work for as long as the aging window allows.
+
+**Analysis.**
+- *Private bookkeeping* — passes.
+- *12 GiB bound on one worker* — passes.
+- *Protect revisited sheets during a long screening* — **strong**. Counts distinguish a genuinely revisited sheet from a scan's one-off.
+- *Bookkeeping cost per request* — **weak**. A counter per entry plus aging work on a schedule, and the aging period has to be tuned against the request rate.
+- *Follow a shift in editors' attention* — **partial**. Counts only reflect the new work once the old work has aged out; the previous production's favourites keep displacing the current one in the meantime.
+- *Robustness to a badly chosen parameter* — **partial**. The aging period and the count threshold interact, and a wrong combination either forgets too eagerly or clings too long.
+- *Explicitness of the warm set* — **partial**. The ranking depends on counters an operator cannot read off the queue order.
+
+**Summary.** Protects a hot set well, but pays for it on every hit and lags a change of production.
+
+### Two-queue replacement (2Q)
+
+**Description.** The cache is split into two queues, each with its own share of the byte budget. A sheet enters the first-use queue when it is first rendered. The first-use queue is ordered by admission, so a screening scan's evictions land on the sheets the scan moved past. When a sheet is asked for again by a request independent of the one that caused its render, it moves into the reused queue, which is ordered by recency and holds a protected share of the budget. Reused sheets leave the cache only after every other reused sheet has been served more recently. The policy needs the request that caused a render to be recognised as that render's single request — including the request that presents the job ticket issued for it — so that the delivery of a freshly rendered sheet is not mistaken for a second request.
+
+**Demonstration.** An editor screens 4,000 clips in order. Each is admitted to the first-use queue and evicts the sheet admitted earliest, so the scan is confined to the first-use queue. The six comparison clips the editor returns to are served again by requests independent of their renders, so they have moved into the reused queue, which the scan cannot touch.
+
+**Analysis.**
+- *Private bookkeeping* — passes; both queues and their recency orders are inside the worker.
+- *12 GiB bound on one worker* — passes; the two queue budgets sum to the cache limit.
+- *Protect revisited sheets during a long screening* — **strong**. A scan's evictions are confined to the first-use queue, and revisited sheets hold a protected share of the budget. The protection depends on the promotion rule: if the delivery of a freshly rendered sheet counted as a second request, every screened clip would be promoted and the two queues would stop meaning anything.
+- *Bookkeeping cost per request* — **partial**. More than one queue's recency order, but no counters and no aging clock, so the per-hit work is a queue lookup and a possible promotion.
+- *Follow a shift in editors' attention* — **partial**. New work enters through the first-use queue's admission order, and sheets that stop being revisited leave the reused queue as they become its least recently served members — but a sheet that was heavily reused ages out only as slowly as the reused queue's recency order moves.
+- *Robustness to a badly chosen parameter* — **weak**. The split between the two budgets is a live parameter: too small a reused budget and the protection collapses into churn, and a too large one starves the first-use queue. The failure is quiet — the cache keeps working, it just stops protecting anything.
+- *Explicitness of the warm set* — **partial**. Two orders rather than one, but both are visible in the worker's own metrics.
+
+**Summary.** Buys scan-resistant protection for revisited sheets, and pays for it with a second queue and a parameter that has to be tuned.
+
+### Editor pinning and explicit priority
+
+**Description.** Instead of inferring importance from access patterns, the cache is told what matters: an editor's comparison set is marked as important while the session lasts, and the cache evicts unmarked sheets first. This is the most direct expression of "protect what I am comparing".
+
+**Demonstration.** The editor opens six clips into a comparison view and the cache keeps those six sheets resident for the duration of the session, dropping everything else as needed.
+
+**Analysis.**
+- *Private bookkeeping* — **eliminated**. Priority has to be signalled from the editor's session through the review API to the worker, and the review API may not pin previews, inspect cache state, or depend on entries remaining warm. The mechanism the option needs is precisely the one the boundary forbids.
+- *12 GiB bound on one worker* — passes.
+- *Protect revisited sheets during a long screening* — **strong**, and exact rather than inferred.
+- *Bookkeeping cost per request* — **partial**; pin state has to be tracked and expires.
+- *Follow a shift in editors' attention* — **strong**, since priority is explicit.
+- *Robustness to a badly chosen parameter* — **strong**; there is no parameter.
+- *Explicitness of the warm set* — **strong**.
+
+**Summary.** Would protect comparison sets precisely, but it needs a signal the worker is not allowed to receive.
+
+## Selection
+
+**Two-queue replacement (2Q) is selected.**
+
+The decision turns on the first and highest-importance criterion, and on the gate that shapes the option set. Against long screenings, single-queue LRU is **weak** — it evicts precisely the sheets an editor will return to — so it cannot be chosen for a workload whose defining feature is the alternation between scanning and comparing. Frequency counts with aging is **strong** on that same criterion and is the closest competitor; it loses on the two moderate criteria that follow from it. Its per-hit cost is **weak**: every hit carries a counter update and periodic aging work on top of the queue bookkeeping, on a worker that already has 24 editors and four concurrent render jobs to serve. And it **follows a shift in editors' attention only partially**: because counts persist until they age out, the previous production's favourites continue to displace the current one's work for the length of the aging window, which is exactly the situation a production change creates. Editor pinning would satisfy the protection criterion better than any other option, and is eliminated by the boundary it would have to cross.
+
+2Q meets the protection criterion in the way this workload demands without paying LFU's per-hit price. Its cost is concentrated where the risk actually lies — in one parameter. That is what makes the third criterion cut against it: a badly chosen split fails **quietly**, with the cache still serving requests while protection quietly lapses. That risk is real and it is why the split between the two budgets is recorded as a tuning parameter to be revisited against observed workloads, rather than a settled number.
+
+Committing to this option has consequences that other parts of the system's intent will lean on:
+
+- **What it forecloses.** The worker keeps no single global "warmest set" order, and it does not keep any popularity statistic — so there is nothing in the worker to answer "which clips are popular this week" except the reused queue's membership. The per-hit cost of maintaining the policy is no longer "one pointer update", and the warm set is no longer explained by a single order.
+- **What it obliges.** The worker must maintain two queues, two byte budgets, a promotion rule, and per-queue eviction orders. The promotion rule has to be specified rather than inferred: the request that causes a render counts once for that sheet, including the request that presents the job ticket issued for that render, and only an independent later request promotes the sheet into the reused queue. A worker that counts the delivery of a freshly rendered sheet as a second request would pass the letter of a naive 2Q description while destroying the protection this decision buys. The split between the two budgets must be revisited when the production mix changes, and the sheet sizes behind the byte budgets must be re-measured if contact sheets change size.
+- **What it enables.** Editors' comparison sets survive a screening, because a scan's evictions are confined to the first-use queue and revisited sheets hold a protected share of the budget — without any new signal crossing the worker's boundary, and without the cache deciding on the review API's behalf that a clip matters.
